     "Analysis proceeds level by level. At the phonological level, clinicians describe substitution and deletion, for instance an English-speaking child who says [wɛd] for red or [tɒp] for stop, instantiating gliding and final consonant deletion. Morphosyntax concerns missing inflections and function words: an agrammatic speaker may produce boy go store, dropping articles, auxiliaries and the past tense marker. Semantics covers paraphasias such as calling an apple an orange, and pragmatics covers turn-taking and repair."
