  "summaryEn": "Sociolinguistics studies the interrelations between language and social structure and social factors, examining how linguistic variation correlates with social class, gender, age, ethnicity and communicative setting, and how language functions in identity construction, language contact and language planning. The quantitative variationist research of Labov and others established the discipline's empirical paradigm, and the field ranges from micro-level variation in speech to macro-level language policy."
