  "summaryEn": "Linguistic typology generalises over structural types and implicational universals through cross-linguistic comparison, asking what a language can possibly be like. It takes the world's languages as its population and asks which structures can occur and which cannot, in contrast with descriptive grammars that study a single language. Using parameters such as word order, morphology and phonology, it establishes classifications of languages and seeks to explain the functional and cognitive motivations behind universals rather than merely listing regularities."
