  "summaryEn": "Deep structure and surface structure are two levels of syntactic representation posited in early generative grammar: deep structure determines the basic semantic interpretation, while surface structure determines phonetic form, the two being linked by transformational rules. The distinction was introduced to account for the systematic correspondences between active and passive sentences and between declaratives and interrogatives, and was later revised into D-structure and S-structure."
