  "summaryEn": "Speech act theory holds that to speak is to act: in uttering a sentence one simultaneously performs such acts as stating, requesting, promising or commanding. Austin distinguished constatives from performatives, and Searle systematised the account into the three-layered structure of locutionary, propositional and illocutionary acts, proposing felicity conditions and a classification of speech acts. The theory became the founding framework of pragmatics."
