  "summaryEn": "Linguistic relativity examines whether and how the structure of a language affects the thought and cognition of its speakers. It derives from the writings of Humboldt, Sapir and Whorf and is commonly known as the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. Its strong version claims that language determines thought, while the weak version claims that language influences attention, memory and classification tendencies; colour terms, spatial reference and temporal metaphor are classic areas of contemporary research."
