  "summaryEn": "The comparative method is the procedure by which historical linguistics compares cognates and sound correspondences across related languages in order to reconstruct their common ancestor and establish genealogical relationship. It rests on the assumption that sound change is regular, and proceeds by identifying cognates, establishing correspondences and reconstructing proto-forms. It is the cornerstone of Indo-European and Sino-Tibetan studies."
