  "summaryEn": "William Labov (1927–2024) was an American linguist and one of the founders of sociolinguistics. Through his surveys of Martha's Vineyard and of New York City department stores he linked linguistic variation to social factors such as age, class, gender and style, created the quantitative paradigm of variationist research, and proposed variable rules and a social mechanism of language change."
