  "summaryEn": "Ferdinand de Saussure (1857-1913) was a Swiss linguist and the founder of modern linguistics. In the Course in General Linguistics he argued that language is a relational system constituted by differences, distinguishing langue from parole, signifier from signified, synchronic from diachronic study, and syntagmatic from paradigmatic relations, thereby laying the theoretical foundations of structuralist linguistics and semiotics."
