   {
    "h": "语言实例",
    "hEn": "Linguistic Examples",
    "p": [
     "格里姆定律可以用日常词例直接观察。拉丁语 pater 与英语 father 相对，印欧语的 p 在日耳曼语中变为 f；拉丁语 trēs、tenuis 与英语 three、thin 相对，t 变为 θ；拉丁语 cornū、centum 与英语 horn、hundred 相对，k 变为 h；拉丁语 duo 与英语 two、拉丁语 genu 与英语 knee，则体现了浊塞音变为清塞音的过程。",
     "规律最初也存在明显例外，例如哥特语 fadar 与拉丁语 pater 对应的辅音并不一致。1875年维尔纳指出，当印欧语的重音不落在该辅音之前的音节时，格里姆定律所预测的清擦音会发生浊化，这一补充规则被称为维尔纳定律。梵语 pitár- 的重音在第二音节，正符合浊化的条件，因此出现了哥特语 fadar 中的 d；维尔纳定律同时也为重建印欧语重音位置提供了证据。"
    ],
    "pEn": [
     "Grimm's Law is directly visible in everyday vocabulary. Latin pater corresponds to English father, the Indo-European p becoming Germanic f; Latin trēs and tenuis correspond to English three and thin, with t becoming θ; Latin cornū and centum correspond to English horn and hundred, with k becoming h. Latin duo beside English two and Latin genu beside English knee show the shift of voiced stops to voiceless ones.",
     "Apparent exceptions soon appeared: Gothic fadar does not match the consonant of Latin pater. In 1875 Karl Verner showed that the voiceless fricatives predicted by Grimm's Law become voiced when the Indo-European accent did not fall on the preceding syllable, a supplement known as Verner's Law. Sanskrit pitár-, accented on the second syllable, meets that condition, which is why Gothic has fadar with d, and the rule also gave linguists a way to reconstruct the position of the Indo-European accent."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "影响与批评",
    "hEn": "Influence and Criticism",
    "p": [
     "格里姆定律是历史比较语言学最早确立的规律性音变之一，它证明语音演变可以脱离个别词例而被概括为规则，这一观念后来在新语法学派手中发展为音律无例外的原则。格林的《德语语法》确立了日耳曼语文学的研究范式，《德语词典》至今仍是德语历史词汇研究的权威依据，格林兄弟的童话集则成为世界文学的一部分。",
     "批评主要针对两点。其一，格里姆定律的例外直到维尔纳定律提出后才获得解释，说明早期比较研究对条件的把握尚不完备。其二，《德国神话学》中不少词源建立在语音对应不够严格的联想之上，被后来的学者视为推测性的民间词源，其结论需要谨慎对待。此外，他编纂词典时偏重文献中的书面用法，对口语材料的利用相对有限。"
    ],
    "pEn": [
     "Grimm's Law was among the first regular sound changes established in comparative linguistics. It showed that phonological development can be stated as a rule rather than recounted word by word, an idea the Neogrammarians later turned into the principle that sound laws admit no exceptions. Deutsche Grammatik established a research paradigm for Germanic philology, the Deutsches Wörterbuch remains an authoritative record of German historical vocabulary, and the brothers' fairy tales became part of world literature.",
     "Criticism has centred on two points. First, the exceptions to Grimm's Law were explained only after Verner's Law was formulated, showing that early comparative work did not yet control all the conditioning factors. Second, many etymologies in Deutsche Mythologie rest on associations that do not meet strict sound correspondences, and later scholars regard them as speculative folk etymology. His dictionary also leans on written sources, making comparatively limited use of spoken usage."
    ]
   }
