    "pEn": [
     "Austronesian languages frequently show verb-initial order and so-called focus or voice systems. In Philippine-type languages verbal affixes mark which argument serves as syntactic subject, so that Tagalog contrasts nagbili 'bought, actor focus' with binili 'bought, object focus'. Reduplication is pervasive, as in Tagalog araw 'day' with araw-araw 'every day'. Many languages distinguish inclusive from exclusive first person plural, as in Tagalog tayo 'we including you' versus kami 'we excluding you'. Cognates reveal the ancestral lexicon: 'eye' is maso in Malagasy and mata in Malay."
    ]
