  "summaryEn": "The Latin alphabet is an alphabet originating in Greek letters by way of the Etruscan alphabet as an intermediary; it had 23 letters in the classical period and was standardised at 26 after J, U and W were added in the Middle Ages. Adopted by most of the world's languages and giving rise to many orthographies, it is also the base symbol set of the International Phonetic Alphabet and Pinyin, and the most widely transmitted writing system today."
